1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:10,519
Howard Lessing wrote the paper
in four nights.

2
00:00:11,089 --> 00:00:16,913
He wrote it at his kitchen table after his wife had
gone to bed, with a glass of Scotch

3
00:00:16,933 --> 00:00:22,622
he did not drink and the blue drive plugged into
the side of his laptop, and he wrote

4
00:00:22,642 --> 00:00:28,532
it better than anything he had written in twenty
years. It was eleven pages long. It had

5
00:00:28,552 --> 00:00:34,510
four figures. Its title was A Novel
Replication-Competent Viral Signature Associated With

6
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:38,204
Extreme Proliferative Repair in
Human Peripheral Blood.

7
00:00:38,773 --> 00:00:40,802
It did not mention whose blood.

8
00:00:41,374 --> 00:00:47,288
He also wrote an email. It was shorter. It was
addressed to a vice-president of research

9
00:00:47,308 --> 00:00:53,087
at Verdant Axis Therapeutics, a man named Simon
Ferris who had once tried to hire him,

10
00:00:53,107 --> 00:00:59,291
and it said that Doctor Lessing had come into
possession of data of extraordinary scientific

11
00:00:59,311 --> 00:01:05,226
and commercial interest, and would welcome a
confidential conversation about his future.

12
00:01:05,796 --> 00:01:11,880
He told himself these were two separate things. The
paper was for science, and for history,

13
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:17,783
and for the prize that had been promised to him at a
conference in nineteen ninety-four.

14
00:01:17,803 --> 00:01:19,795
The email was for his pension.

15
00:01:20,365 --> 00:01:26,170
He saved them both as drafts. He would send them on
Tuesday night, from the lab, after

16
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:28,609
the preprint server's weekly update.

17
00:01:30,379 --> 00:01:35,645
On Monday morning, the Director held his weekly
meeting in the break room, and Howard

18
00:01:35,665 --> 00:01:40,994
Lessing sat at the far end of the table and tried to
look like a man who had spent the

19
00:01:41,014 --> 00:01:42,114
weekend gardening.

20
00:01:42,683 --> 00:01:48,444
It did not work. It would not have worked on anyone
paying attention. It certainly did

21
00:01:48,464 --> 00:01:54,359
not work on a man who could now read the human face
the way other people read subtitles.

22
00:01:54,930 --> 00:02:00,881
Guilt, in the crease beside his eye. Fear, in the
way he held his cup with both hands.

23
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:06,921
And under them both, a thing the Cold-Read had no
single word for — a kind of trembling

24
00:02:06,941 --> 00:02:12,892
exultation, like a man standing on the edge of a
high diving board, about to jump, and

25
00:02:12,912 --> 00:02:15,044
sure the water would catch him.

26
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:20,997
The Director said nothing. He talked about the
solvent store, and the Shenzhen licence,

27
00:02:21,017 --> 00:02:24,226
and the new coffee. He did not look
at Lessing once.

28
00:02:25,798 --> 00:02:29,115
At noon, Hale came to his office
and closed the door.

29
00:02:29,805 --> 00:02:35,396
Doctor Lessing has two drafts saved to the building's
mail server, Director. One is addressed

30
00:02:35,416 --> 00:02:40,706
to a preprint service. One to a Simon Ferris, at
Verdant Axis. He's also copied fourteen

31
00:02:40,726 --> 00:02:44,810
gigabytes from analyzer three to a
removable drive. I have the logs.

32
00:02:45,330 --> 00:02:46,483
Of course you do.

33
00:02:47,003 --> 00:02:48,889
Shall I take the drive?

34
00:02:49,409 --> 00:02:50,584
No. Let him bring it.

35
00:02:52,305 --> 00:02:57,415
The argument happened on the drive home that
night. It was the first real one.

36
00:02:58,105 --> 00:02:59,766
He is a traitor.

37
00:03:00,286 --> 00:03:03,078
He's a disappointed old man with
a thumb drive.

38
00:03:03,599 --> 00:03:09,930
He stole from you. He is going to sell you to your
enemy. In my time a man like that was

39
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:15,270
not dismissed. He was made an example. So that
the next man would not try.

40
00:03:15,789 --> 00:03:20,993
In your time you had a secret police and a country
that didn't ask questions. Here, if

41
00:03:21,013 --> 00:03:26,156
Howard Lessing disappears, his wife calls the
Wilmington police on Wednesday morning,

42
00:03:26,176 --> 00:03:31,562
and the Wilmington police call his colleagues, and
one of his colleagues is Nadia Okafor,

43
00:03:31,582 --> 00:03:34,052
who is already measuring me for a coffin.

44
00:03:34,573 --> 00:03:37,305
Then do it so that nobody calls.

45
00:03:37,824 --> 00:03:42,789
Listen to me very carefully. We are not doing
that. Not now. Not for this.

46
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:46,415
For a while there was only the road.

47
00:03:47,106 --> 00:03:48,238
Then what?

48
00:03:48,758 --> 00:03:54,587
A dead man is a problem. A frightened man is an
asset. I don't want Howard Lessing gone.

49
00:03:54,607 --> 00:03:55,650
I want him mine.

50
00:03:56,170 --> 00:04:00,758
A man who is afraid of you is not
yours. He is only afraid.

51
00:04:01,278 --> 00:04:05,284
That's what you never understood. For most
men, it's the same thing.

52
00:04:07,304 --> 00:04:13,174
On Tuesday night, at eight minutes to midnight,
Howard Lessing sat alone in lab two with

53
00:04:13,194 --> 00:04:17,257
the blue drive plugged in and both
drafts open on the screen.

54
00:04:17,826 --> 00:04:23,859
The building was empty. He had checked. Tomás had
left at six, Evelyn at ten — early,

55
00:04:23,879 --> 00:04:29,911
for her. Walter was off. The new security man,
Hale, was supposedly doing rounds, but

56
00:04:29,931 --> 00:04:35,607
Lessing had not seen him since eight, and had
decided that the new man was lazy.

57
00:04:36,177 --> 00:04:42,016
His finger was on the trackpad. The cursor was on the
button. He had imagined this moment

58
00:04:42,036 --> 00:04:47,678
for thirty years, and in all thirty years it had
looked like a stage in Stockholm, and

59
00:04:47,698 --> 00:04:53,736
not like a dim lab in an industrial park with a
flickering tube and the smell of old bleach.

60
00:04:54,305 --> 00:04:55,439
He took a breath.

61
00:04:57,129 --> 00:04:58,710
It's a good paper, Howard.

62
00:04:59,230 --> 00:05:03,720
Lessing made a sound that was not quite a
word, and spun the chair around.

63
00:05:04,290 --> 00:05:09,799
The Director was standing directly behind him. Close
enough to touch. Lessing had been

64
00:05:09,819 --> 00:05:15,394
facing the lab door, which was the only door, which
was forty feet away across an empty

65
00:05:15,414 --> 00:05:20,731
room, and which had not opened. He knew it had not
opened. He had been watching it.

66
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:23,638
How — I didn't — how long have you —

67
00:05:24,158 --> 00:05:29,915
The figures are excellent. Figure three especially. The
division curve. You've found something

68
00:05:29,935 --> 00:05:35,447
nobody has ever found, and you've described it
clearly, and you've drawn exactly the right

69
00:05:35,467 --> 00:05:37,599
conclusion. It's a very good paper.

70
00:05:38,598 --> 00:05:39,816
It's also my blood.

71
00:05:41,836 --> 00:05:45,456
Lessing did not understand him at
first. Then he did.

72
00:05:46,026 --> 00:05:52,339
Control Seven. The calibration sample. The tube the
Director had handed Tomás with his

73
00:05:52,359 --> 00:05:53,002
own hand.

74
00:05:53,692 --> 00:05:59,005
That's — no. That's not possible. Those cells —
nothing human does that. Nothing —

75
00:05:59,524 --> 00:06:01,695
The Director took off his sunglasses.

76
00:06:03,266 --> 00:06:04,672
His eyes were red.

77
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:11,317
Not bloodshot. Red, the way a coal is red. And the
pupils were not round. They were narrow

78
00:06:11,337 --> 00:06:17,414
and vertical, like a cat's, like a snake's, and they
widened, very slightly, as they found

79
00:06:17,434 --> 00:06:20,056
Howard Lessing's face in the lamplight.

80
00:06:20,627 --> 00:06:26,648
Lessing had been a scientist for thirty-four years.
He did not believe in monsters. And

81
00:06:26,668 --> 00:06:32,620
in that moment, looking up into those eyes from a
cheap office chair, some part of his

82
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:38,593
mind older than science — older than language —
understood exactly what it was looking

83
00:06:38,613 --> 00:06:40,745
at, and screamed at him to run.

84
00:06:41,314 --> 00:06:44,610
He did not run. He could not make
his legs work.

85
00:06:46,802 --> 00:06:48,808
Nothing human does that. You're right.

86
00:06:50,208 --> 00:06:54,689
So let me tell you what happens next, Howard,
and then you're going to choose.

87
00:06:55,209 --> 00:07:00,846
He pulled a second chair around, and sat down
facing Lessing, knee to knee, the way a

88
00:07:00,866 --> 00:07:02,775
doctor sits when he has news.

89
00:07:03,464 --> 00:07:08,839
If you press that button, the preprint goes live
tomorrow at nine. It will be the most-read

90
00:07:08,859 --> 00:07:14,114
paper in the world for about eleven days. Then
someone will ask for the sample. And there

91
00:07:14,134 --> 00:07:19,212
won't be one. The analyzer's memory was wiped this
afternoon — a firmware update, very

92
00:07:19,232 --> 00:07:24,310
routine, Gus has the ticket. Tomás remembers running
a calibration that failed because

93
00:07:24,330 --> 00:07:29,467
the reagents were bad. He's told Dana so, in
writing. There's no Control Seven. There's

94
00:07:29,487 --> 00:07:34,624
no data. There's a fifty-eight-year-old scientist
who was passed over his whole career,

95
00:07:34,644 --> 00:07:39,663
who stole files from his employer, emailed a
competitor for a job the same night, and

96
00:07:39,683 --> 00:07:43,634
fabricated the most extraordinary result
in the history of biology.

97
00:07:44,834 --> 00:07:50,621
Simon Ferris will forward your email to Verdant's
lawyers inside an hour. Your pension

98
00:07:50,641 --> 00:07:56,360
goes. Your name goes. And every obituary they
eventually write about you will mention

99
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:58,182
it in the second paragraph.

100
00:07:59,702 --> 00:08:01,242
And — the other choice?

101
00:08:02,362 --> 00:08:07,667
You delete them. Both. Yourself, now, while I watch.
You give me the drive. And you stay.

102
00:08:07,687 --> 00:08:12,813
You keep your job, and your salary goes up in
January, and one day — not soon, but one

103
00:08:12,833 --> 00:08:17,120
day — you get to see what that blood becomes.
You get to be in the room.

104
00:08:18,841 --> 00:08:24,301
Somewhere below them, very faint through the floor,
a compressor cycled. A long, slow

105
00:08:24,321 --> 00:08:24,753
breath.

106
00:08:25,322 --> 00:08:30,697
Lessing did not know what was in the basement. He
would not know for years. But he heard

107
00:08:30,717 --> 00:08:36,092
the sound, and he saw the Director glance down toward
it, just once, with something that

108
00:08:36,112 --> 00:08:41,303
looked almost like affection — and for the rest of
his life, when he could not sleep,

109
00:08:41,323 --> 00:08:43,633
Howard Lessing would hear that breath.

110
00:08:45,703 --> 00:08:47,490
He turned back to the laptop.

111
00:08:48,059 --> 00:08:53,314
He deleted the paper. He deleted the email. He
emptied the trash folder without being

112
00:08:53,334 --> 00:08:58,650
asked, and then he opened the mail server's drafts
folder and deleted it from there as

113
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:03,986
well, because he was a thorough man, and because he
wanted — he did not know why — for

114
00:09:04,006 --> 00:09:07,708
the thing in front of him to see that
he was being thorough.

115
00:09:08,279 --> 00:09:13,776
Then he pulled the blue drive out of the laptop and
held it out. His hand was shaking

116
00:09:13,796 --> 00:09:17,475
so badly that the drive rattled
against his wedding ring.

117
00:09:18,046 --> 00:09:23,973
The Director took it between his thumb and
forefinger, and squeezed. The casing split.

118
00:09:23,993 --> 00:09:26,255
The chip inside folded like foil.

119
00:09:26,946 --> 00:09:31,884
Thank you, Howard. Go home. Kiss your wife. Come in
at nine tomorrow. Don't be late.

120
00:09:33,905 --> 00:09:39,292
Howard Lessing walked out to the parking lot, and
put one hand on the roof of his car,

121
00:09:39,312 --> 00:09:40,990
and was sick on the tarmac.

122
00:09:41,559 --> 00:09:47,238
Then he drove home, very carefully, under the speed
limit the whole way, and got into

123
00:09:47,258 --> 00:09:52,400
bed beside his wife, and lay on his back in the
dark until the sky went grey.

124
00:09:52,971 --> 00:09:57,077
He came in at nine. He was not late. He
was never late again.

125
00:09:58,647 --> 00:10:04,771
He was never loyal, either. That is important, and it
is the thing the Director misjudged.

126
00:10:04,791 --> 00:10:10,710
Howard Lessing would never love Umbrella, or believe
in it, or want it to win. He would

127
00:10:10,730 --> 00:10:16,649
only be afraid of it. And fear, in a man with
nothing left to lose, has a half-life. It

128
00:10:16,669 --> 00:10:18,834
decays. Slowly. Like everything.

129
00:10:21,653 --> 00:10:24,101
Assessment. Traitor contained.

130
00:10:28,208 --> 00:10:30,225
Level up. Level four.

131
00:10:32,514 --> 00:10:35,657
Three into Intelligence. Two into Defence.

132
00:10:36,346 --> 00:10:38,586
You have made a man who hates you.

133
00:10:39,106 --> 00:10:43,929
I've made a man who obeys me. Hate's a hobby.
It doesn't show up to work.

134
00:10:44,450 --> 00:10:48,569
In my experience, hate shows up to
everything eventually.

135
00:10:49,087 --> 00:10:54,592
In your experience, you were surrounded by men who
hated you and you had them shot. And

136
00:10:54,612 --> 00:10:55,863
then you still lost.

137
00:10:57,384 --> 00:11:01,490
We shall see who is right about
Howard Lessing.

138
00:11:02,010 --> 00:11:04,086
They would. It would take three years.

139
00:11:06,155 --> 00:11:08,506
Evelyn Marsh noticed the next morning.

140
00:11:09,076 --> 00:11:14,297
She noticed because she noticed everything about the
building now — she had started to

141
00:11:14,317 --> 00:11:19,659
think of it, privately, as hers. Howard Lessing had
come in at nine exactly. He had said

142
00:11:19,679 --> 00:11:24,838
good morning to everyone, including Tomás, whom he
had not said good morning to since

143
00:11:24,858 --> 00:11:30,078
two thousand twenty-two. He was working on a routine
assay with the concentration of a

144
00:11:30,098 --> 00:11:35,379
man defusing a bomb, and every few minutes, without
seeming to know he was doing it, he

145
00:11:35,399 --> 00:11:36,597
looked at the floor.

146
00:11:37,288 --> 00:11:38,395
Is Howard all right?

147
00:11:38,916 --> 00:11:40,385
Howard's had a revelation.

148
00:11:40,904 --> 00:11:42,222
What kind of revelation?

149
00:11:42,743 --> 00:11:43,896
The religious kind.

150
00:11:45,216 --> 00:11:50,961
She looked at him for a long moment. He looked back,
perfectly pleasant, sunglasses on,

151
00:11:50,981 --> 00:11:52,420
and did not elaborate.

152
00:11:52,990 --> 00:11:58,417
A more cautious person might have been frightened by
that answer. Evelyn Marsh was not

153
00:11:58,437 --> 00:12:03,928
a cautious person. She went back to her bench
feeling something she would not have been

154
00:12:03,948 --> 00:12:08,488
able to name, but which was, if she had been
honest, very close to envy.

155
00:12:10,558 --> 00:12:16,752
Across town, in the eleven-person newsroom of the
Wilmington Ledger, Hannah Price had

156
00:12:16,772 --> 00:12:22,016
found the name of the man who ran Verdant
Axis's three good trial sites.

157
00:12:22,586 --> 00:12:29,002
It was a private clinic network in Maryland. Its
registered owner was a limited partnership.

158
00:12:29,022 --> 00:12:33,899
The limited partnership's general partner
was a man named Curtis Vane.

159
00:12:34,470 --> 00:12:37,458
Curtis Vane was Graham Tolliver's
brother-in-law.

160
00:12:38,028 --> 00:12:43,253
She sat back in her chair and looked at the board
for a long time. Then she picked up

161
00:12:43,273 --> 00:12:48,375
her phone, and scrolled to a number she had saved
as Umbrella, and did not call it.

162
00:12:48,946 --> 00:12:55,009
She was not a fool. She knew she was being used. She
had known it in the diner. The question

163
00:12:55,029 --> 00:13:00,762
she could not stop asking herself — the question
that would drive the next two years of

164
00:13:00,782 --> 00:13:04,400
her life — was not whether Albert
Wesker was using her.

165
00:13:04,970 --> 00:13:06,509
It was how he had known.
